\title{Sorted-Chamber Geometry of Generalized Parking Profiles\\and Short Modular-Kernel Witnesses}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 22, 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study generalized parking functions through positive nondecreasing cumulative profiles. A vector is admitted when a permutation places its coordinates in nondecreasing order and the coordinate of each rank lies below the corresponding profile bound. This decomposes the symmetric parking region into sorted chambers with elementary rank inequalities. We prove that deleting the same rank from a sorted admitted vector and its profile preserves admission in one lower dimension, establish monotonicity under profile enlargement, and show that $a\mapsto1+t(a-1)$ transports admitted vectors and profiles. We also derive a universal coordinate bound and a short modular-kernel theorem: for a length-$N$ profile with largest bound $B$, an $m\times N$ integer matrix $A$, and $q>0$, the inequality $q^m<(2B+1)^N$ guarantees nonzero $z\in\Z^N$ with $Az\equiv0\pmod q$ and $\lVert z\rVert_\infty\le2B$. Rank deletion is chamberwise; identifying an entire labelled-coordinate slice with one lower-dimensional parking polytope additionally requires a value-dependent gluing theorem.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
For a positive integer vector $\mathbf b=(b_1,\ldots,b_n)$, define cumulative bounds $P_i=\sum_{k=1}^i b_k$. A generalized $\mathbf b$-parking function is a positive integer vector whose nondecreasing rearrangement $y_1\le\cdots\le y_n$ satisfies $y_i\le P_i$. The associated point sets and convex hulls lead naturally to lattice slices, enumeration, and Ehrhart questions, but permutation symmetry can obscure their elementary rank structure.

We use cumulative profiles as primitive objects. In any fixed coordinate ordering, admission becomes
\[
1\le y_1\le\cdots\le y_n,\qquad y_i\le P_i.
\]
Such a region is a \emph{sorted chamber}. This viewpoint makes rank deletion and affine transport transparent. It also supplies a largest-bound cube, which we connect to bounded modular collision searches and short-integer-solution problems.

Our slice result is deliberately local. Fixing a labelled coordinate may place it at different ranks in adjacent chambers. Rank deletion exactly describes every fixed-rank piece, but a separate gluing theorem is needed to identify their union with a single lower-dimensional polytope.

\section{Profiles and sorted chambers}

\begin{definition}[Cumulative parking profile]
A cumulative parking profile of length $n$ is a positive nondecreasing integer sequence $P=(P_1,\ldots,P_n)$:
\[
0<P_1\le\cdots\le P_n.
\]
Every positive parameter vector $\mathbf b$ determines such a profile by $P_i=b_1+\cdots+b_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admission]
A vector $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\in\Z_{>0}^n$ is \emph{admitted by $P$} if some permutation $\sigma$ satisfies
\[
x_{\sigma(1)}\le\cdots\le x_{\sigma(n)},\qquad x_{\sigma(i)}\le P_i
\]
for every $i$. Equivalently, its nondecreasing rearrangement $x_{(1)},\ldots,x_{(n)}$ satisfies $1\le x_{(i)}\le P_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Sorted chamber]
For a fixed permutation $\sigma$, the corresponding sorted chamber is the set cut out by
\[
1\le x_{\sigma(1)}\le\cdots\le x_{\sigma(n)},\qquad x_{\sigma(i)}\le P_i.
\]
The admitted set is the union of all such chambers, with overlaps where coordinates tie.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Sorted-chamber representation]
Every vector admitted by $P$ has a nondecreasing rearrangement $y$ satisfying $1\le y_i\le P_i$, and $y$ is obtained from the original vector by a coordinate permutation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Take the permutation in the definition of admission and set $y_i=x_{\sigma(i)}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Rank deletion and chamberwise slices}
For $r\in\{1,\ldots,n+1\}$, write
\[
P^{\widehat r}=(P_1,\ldots,P_{r-1},P_{r+1},\ldots,P_{n+1}).
\]
It remains a positive nondecreasing profile.

\begin{theorem}[Rank-Deletion Slice Theorem]
Let $P=(P_1,\ldots,P_{n+1})$ be a cumulative profile and let $y_1\le\cdots\le y_{n+1}$ satisfy $1\le y_i\le P_i$. For any rank $r$, delete $y_r$ and $P_r$. The shortened vector $y^{\widehat r}$ is admitted by the shortened profile $P^{\widehat r}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Deleting an entry from a nondecreasing sequence preserves order. Every surviving coordinate remains paired with its original surviving bound, so positivity and all inequalities remain valid. The identity ordering witnesses admission after reindexing.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Iterated deletion]
Deleting any collection of ranks from a sorted admitted vector and the same ranks from its profile preserves admission.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the rank-deletion theorem repeatedly.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Local versus global slices]
If a labelled coordinate $x_j=c$ occupies rank $r$ in a chamber, deleting it gives profile $P^{\widehat r}$. The same value may occupy different ranks in other chambers. Thus the theorem identifies every rank piece but does not assert that the union is one profile polytope. That stronger conclusion requires a value-dependent rule and a proof that adjacent chamber pieces glue.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
For $P=(2,5,7,10)$ and $y=(1,4,6,9)$, deleting rank $3$ gives $P^{\widehat3}=(2,5,10)$ and $y^{\widehat3}=(1,4,9)$, which remains admitted.
\end{example}

\section{Monotonicity}
\begin{theorem}[Profile Monotonicity]
Let $P,Q$ be length-$n$ profiles with $P_i\le Q_i$ for all $i$. Every vector admitted by $P$ is admitted by $Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A permutation witnessing $x_{\sigma(i)}\le P_i$ also witnesses $x_{\sigma(i)}\le Q_i$ by transitivity. Sortedness is unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nested admitted sets]
Coordinatewise ordered profiles induce nested admitted sets; increasing profile bounds cannot remove an admitted vector.
\end{corollary}

\section{Affine dilation about the all-ones vector}
\begin{definition}[Integral affine dilation]
For $t\in\Z_{\ge0}$ and $a\in\Z_{>0}$, set
\[
D_t(a)=1+t(a-1).
\]
Apply $D_t$ coordinatewise to vectors and profiles. Thus $D_t(x)=\one+t(x-\one)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Order preservation]
If $1\le a\le b$ and $t\ge0$, then $1\le D_t(a)\le D_t(b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Subtract $1$, multiply the resulting nonnegative inequality by $t$, and add $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Affine Dilation Theorem]
If $x$ is admitted by $P$, then $D_t(x)$ is admitted by $D_t(P)$ for every $t\in\Z_{\ge0}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose a permutation sorting $x$. Order preservation shows that it also sorts $D_t(x)$ and transforms each inequality $1\le x_{\sigma(i)}\le P_i$ into $1\le D_t(x_{\sigma(i)})\le D_t(P_i)$. The transformed bounds remain positive and nondecreasing.
\end{proof}

The cases $t=0$ and $t=1$ respectively collapse everything to the all-ones vector and leave everything fixed. Moreover, $D_s(D_t(a))=D_{st}(a)$.

\begin{proposition}[Compatibility of deletion and dilation]
For every rank $r$,
\[
D_t(P^{\widehat r})=(D_t(P))^{\widehat r},
\]
and the same identity holds for sorted vectors.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Both sides apply $D_t$ to exactly the coordinates whose ranks differ from $r$.
\end{proof}

\section{Bounding cubes}
\begin{theorem}[Universal Coordinate Bound]
If $P$ has length $N$ and $x$ is admitted by $P$, then
\[
1\le x_j\le P_N
\]
for every coordinate $j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
After sorting, a coordinate at rank $i$ is at most $P_i\le P_N$. A permutation changes no coordinate values, and positivity is part of admission.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cube containment]
The admitted lattice set lies in $[1,P_N]^N\cap\Z^N$, and its convex hull lies in the real cube $[1,P_N]^N$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Short modular-kernel witnesses}
Let $A\in\Z^{m\times N}$ and $q>0$. The syndrome map is $S_A(u)=Au\pmod q$, with values in $(\Z/q\Z)^m$, a set of size $q^m$.

\begin{lemma}[Bounded-box collision]
Let $B\ge0$. If
\[
q^m<(2B+1)^N,
\]
then some nonzero $z\in\Z^N$ satisfies $Az\equiv0\pmod q$ and $\lVert z\rVert_\infty\le2B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The box $[-B,B]^N\cap\Z^N$ has $(2B+1)^N$ points but only $q^m$ possible syndromes. Two distinct points $u,v$ have the same syndrome. Their difference $z=u-v$ is nonzero, lies in the modular kernel, and has $|z_i|\le2B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parking-Box Modular-Kernel Theorem]
Let $P$ be a length-$N$ profile with largest bound $B=P_N$. If $A\in\Z^{m\times N}$, $q>0$, and
\[
q^m<(2P_N+1)^N,
\]
then there exists nonzero $z\in\Z^N$ such that
\[
Az\equiv0\pmod q,\qquad |z_i|\le2P_N\quad(1\le i\le N).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the bounded-box collision lemma with $B=P_N$.
\end{proof}

The theorem uses a profile-derived search radius but does not claim that the witness is itself a parking vector. It is a matrix-agnostic baseline. A profile-sensitive improvement would count structured parking points or their differences and exploit compatibility between matrix columns and rank order.

\section{Algorithms}
\paragraph{Admission testing.}
Validate that $P$ is positive and nondecreasing, sort $x$, and check $1\le x_{(i)}\le P_i$. The cost is $O(n\log n)$ comparisons plus $O(n)$ checks.

\paragraph{Rank deletion.}
Validate the sorted inequalities and remove rank $r$ from both lists. Explicit output costs $O(n)$ time and space.

\paragraph{Affine transport.}
Apply $a\mapsto1+t(a-1)$ independently to all coordinates and bounds. This uses $O(n)$ arithmetic operations.

\paragraph{Modular collision search.}
Enumerate $u\in[-B,B]^N$, compute $Au\bmod q$, and store the first point for each syndrome. On repetition, return the difference. Direct computation costs at most $O(mN(2B+1)^N)$ modular operations and stores at most $\min(q^m,(2B+1)^N)$ syndromes.

\section{Applications and discussion}
Rank deletion gives a recursive local model for lattice slices and higher-codimension intersections when ranks are tracked. Profile monotonicity permits comparison and incremental enumeration. Affine dilation identifies the correct center for positive parking coordinates and suggests a possible integer-decomposition property, although such a property is not established here.

The modular theorem illustrates how combinatorial geometry can determine an arithmetic search scale. The surrounding cube discards much of the parking region's rank structure, but its simplicity yields a universal guarantee. Future improvements may use exact parking counts and structured matrix hypotheses to reduce the infinity radius.

\section{Future work}
Four directions are immediate. First, determine an explicit value-dependent profile and gluing theorem for labelled-coordinate slices. Second, seek a shelling of sorted chambers whose local contributions explain coefficientwise positivity of lattice-count and Ehrhart polynomials. Third, construct rankwise decompositions of lattice points under affine dilation. Fourth, identify modular matrices for which collisions inside a parking-profile region outperform surrounding-cube collisions.

\section{Conclusion}
Cumulative profiles reveal the ordered structure hidden by permutation symmetry. In each sorted chamber, matched rank deletion preserves admission; profile enlargement creates nested regions; and affine scaling about $\one$ transports the complete inequality system. The largest profile bound then gives a rigorous radius for short modular-kernel witnesses through a finite pigeonhole argument. These results provide exact local operations and a cryptographic baseline while isolating the central global problem of gluing rank-dependent slice pieces.

\end{document}

